-- title:
--   Cyclotomic inertia action on U^N of the connected Tate module
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $O$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain) equipped with ring homomorphisms into an algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of $\mathbb Q$ and into $\mathbb F_p=\mathrm{ZMod}\,p$. Let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that every element of $O$ maps into $P$, and such that an element of $O$ dies in $\mathbb F_p$ exactly when its image has $P$-valuation $<1$. Let $H$ be a $p$-divisible group of height $h$ over $O$ in the sense of the structure [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): levels $H.\mathrm{level}\,v$ that are commutative cocommutative Hopf $O$-algebras, finite and free of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective bialgebra transition maps $H.\mathrm{level}(v+1)\to H.\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals. Let $u=(u_v)$ be a family of bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels commuting with the transitions, $\mathrm{transition}_v\circ u_{v+1}=u_v\circ \mathrm{transition}_v$, and let $U$ be a $\mathbb Z_p$-linear endomorphism of the Tate module $T=\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,(H.\mathrm{Points}\,\overline{\mathbb Q})$ — the group of sequences $(x_n)$ of points with $p^nx_n=0$ and $px_{n+1}=x_n$ — which is induced by $u$ in the sense that whenever the $n$-th component of $x$ is the image of a point $f\colon H.\mathrm{level}\,w\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ under `pointsMkAdd`, the $n$-th component of $Ux$ is the image of $f\circ u_w$. Assume further the hypothesis `hFV` on the special fibre: for every bialgebra endomorphism $F_k$ of $\mathbb F_p\otimes_O H.\mathrm{level}\,1$ given by $x\mapsto x^p$ and every algebra endomorphism $F_D$ of the Cartier dual $\mathrm{CartierDual}(\mathbb F_p,\mathbb F_p\otimes_O H.\mathrm{level}\,1)$ given by $\psi\mapsto\psi^p$, there are $n\in\mathbb N$ and bialgebra endomorphisms $a,b$ of that Cartier dual with the $n$-th composition power of $\mathrm{CartierDual.map}(\mathrm{id}\otimes u_1)$ equal to the convolution product of $F_D\circ a$ and $b\circ\mathrm{CartierDual.map}\,F_k$. Then there is an $N\in\mathbb N$ such that for every $y\in T$ lying in the connected part — each component of $y$ is represented by a point $f$ at some level $w$ with $P$-valuation of $f(a)-\varepsilon(a)$ less than $1$ for all $a$ in that level, $\varepsilon$ the counit composed with $O\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ — there exists $z\in T$, again in the connected part, such that for all $\tau\in\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ lying in the inertia subgroup of $P$ over $\mathbb Q$ (the image of $P.\mathrm{inertiaSubgroup}$ in the automorphism group) and every $O$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ agreeing pointwise with $\tau$, the action of $\tau'$ on $T$ sends $U^Ny-p\,z$ to $\chi_{\mathrm{cyc}}(\tau)\cdot(U^Ny-p\,z)$, where $\chi_{\mathrm{cyc}}$ is the $p$-adic cyclotomic character.
--
--   This is an abstract form of the slope principle used by Mazur–Wiles and Wiles: an endomorphism of a $p$-divisible group whose reduction has a power in the ideal generated by Frobenius and Verschiebung acts on the connected Tate module, modulo $p$ times that module, through the cyclotomic character of the inertia group. It is applied to the Tate module of the Jacobian of a modular curve in [`ModularCurve.tateGaloisRep_smul_sub_eq_cyclotomicCharacter_smul_of_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn_cornerSubmodule_tateModule_jH_of_ordinary`](thm.html#ModularCurve.tateGaloisRep_smul_sub_eq_cyclotomicCharacter_smul_of_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn_cornerSubmodule_tateModule_jH_of_ordinary), where the hypothesis on the special fibre comes from the Eichler–Shimura relation.
